steps.body:
The lesson *How a Computer Runs Your Program* said that the **kernel** is the only code allowed to use the hardware, and that running a program creates a **process**. The CPU enforces that rule. Your code runs in **user mode**, where the CPU refuses the instructions that touch devices or another process's memory. To print, open a file, or get more memory, a program makes a **system call**: it puts a call number and its arguments in registers and runs one special instruction, `syscall` on x86-64 or `svc` on ARM64. The CPU switches to **kernel mode**, the kernel checks the request and does the work, and control comes back with a result.

Linux and macOS share POSIX, a standard set of C functions that wrap system calls, many of them in `<unistd.h>`:

```cpp
#include <unistd.h>

const char msg[] = "hello from the kernel\n";
const ssize_t n = write(1, msg, sizeof msg - 1);  // sizeof counts the final '\0'
std::cout << "write took " << n << " bytes\n";
std::cout << "process id " << getpid() << '\n';
```

- `write(fd, bytes, count)` hands bytes to the kernel and returns how many it took, or -1 on an error, so its type is `ssize_t`, POSIX's signed size. Descriptor 1 is standard output, the next part's subject.
- `getpid()` returns the process id, the number the kernel knows this process by. Your own machine gives a new one every run.

`std::cout` ends in the same `write`. Windows has its own API, with `WriteFile` and `GetCurrentProcessId` from `<windows.h>`.

A system call costs far more than a function call, because the CPU switches modes and the kernel checks every argument. On this Mac, one `write` of a short line to `/dev/null` took about 250 ns, against about 15 ns a line when the C library buffered the lines into big blocks, rough numbers. That's why low-latency code keeps system calls out of its hot loop. On Linux, `strace ./prog` lists every system call a program makes, and macOS has `dtruss`, which needs System Integrity Protection turned off.

example.code:
#include <string>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <vector>

// Hands the whole string to the kernel in one system call.
long writeOut(const std::string& text) {
    return write(1, text.data(), text.size());
}

int main() {
    const std::vector<std::string> quotes = {"AAPL 189.84\n", "MSFT 411.22\n", "SPY 512.10\n"};
    long bytes = 0;
    int calls = 0;
    for (const std::string& line : quotes) {
        bytes += writeOut(line);
        ++calls;
    }
    writeOut("wrote " + std::to_string(bytes) + " bytes in " + std::to_string(calls) + " system calls\n");
    return 0;
}

