steps.body:
`main`'s return value is the **exit status** that `echo $?` shows, as *From Source to Executable: Preprocess, Compile, Link* demonstrated. Underneath, the C library flushes `std::cout` and makes the system call that ends the process, and the kernel hands the number to the parent, usually your shell. Only the low 8 bits survive, so a status runs from 0 to 255 and `return 256;` reads as success. `EXIT_SUCCESS` and `EXIT_FAILURE` from `<cstdlib>` name the usual two, and `std::exit(code)` ends the program the same way from inside any function. Many tools return 2 for wrong usage and 1 for other failures, and `make && ./backtest` runs the backtest only when the build returned 0:

```cpp error
#include <cstdlib>
#include <iostream>

void loadConfig() {
    std::cout << "loading config\n";
    std::cerr << "error: config missing\n";
    std::exit(EXIT_FAILURE);  // flushes std::cout and ends the process, status 1
}

int main() {
    loadConfig();
    std::cout << "never printed\n";
    return 0;
}
```

The parent also passes **arguments**. `int main(int argc, char* argv[])` receives their count and an array of C strings, where `argv[0]` is the program's own name, so `std::vector<std::string> args(argv + 1, argv + argc);` copies just the arguments into strings. The sandbox passes none, so `argc` is 1 there. **Environment variables** are named strings each process inherits from its parent, and `NAME=value ./prog` sets one for a single run. `std::getenv("NAME")` returns a `const char*`, or `nullptr` when the name isn't set, so test it before you use it.

A POSIX shell starts a program with two system calls: `fork` copies the shell's process, and the copy calls `exec`, which swaps in your program but keeps its descriptors and environment (`posix_spawn` does both, and Windows has `CreateProcess`). A `|` joins two processes with a **pipe**, a kernel buffer one writes and the other reads, and processes that pass data at high rates share memory instead, with `shm_open` and the next lesson's `mmap`. *Processes Working Together: fork, Pipes, and Shared Memory*, at the end of this section, does all of it from C++.

Here is this part's tool on this Mac, built with a `main` that ends in `return runOrder(args, std::cout, std::cerr);`:

```text
$ ./order AAPL 100
buy 100 AAPL for paper
$ echo $?
0
$ ORDER_ACCOUNT=desk7 ./order MSFT 50
buy 50 MSFT for desk7
$ ./order AAPL
usage: order SYMBOL QTY
$ echo $?
2
$ ./order AAPL 1O0 2> errors.txt
$ echo $?
1
```

`std::cout` and `std::cerr` are both `std::ostream` objects, so a `std::ostream&` parameter accepts either. That lets the sandbox example pass `std::cout` for both and check every message.

example.code:
#include <cstdlib>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

// The tool's logic takes its arguments and streams as parameters, so tests can call it.
int runOrder(const std::vector<std::string>& args, std::ostream& out, std::ostream& err) {
    if (args.size() != 2) {
        err << "usage: order SYMBOL QTY\n";
        return 2;
    }
    const std::string& qty = args[1];
    for (char c : qty) {
        if (c < '0' || c > '9') {
            err << "error: bad quantity " << qty << '\n';
            return 1;
        }
    }
    const char* account = std::getenv("ORDER_ACCOUNT");  // nullptr when not set
    out << "buy " << qty << ' ' << args[0] << " for " << (account != nullptr ? account : "paper") << '\n';
    return 0;
}

int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    std::cout << "argc here: " << argc << '\n';
    // The sandbox passes no arguments, so try three command lines by hand.
    const std::vector<std::vector<std::string>> runs = {{"AAPL", "100"}, {"AAPL"}, {"AAPL", "1O0"}};
    for (const auto& args : runs) {
        const int status = runOrder(args, std::cout, std::cout);
        std::cout << "exit status " << status << '\n';
    }
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}

